+NOTES = r"""
+STEP 0 and the paper. Section 2 defines the free algebra K<X>, two-sided and
+right ideals, noncommutative monomials, compatible polynomials, and the
+elimination property. Section 3 formulates the motivating search exactly as:
+find a polynomial a*x*b-c in the ideal generated by the known identities; its
+worked example uses a degree-5 approximation with 263 compatible right-ideal
+generators (3485 without quiver compatibility). Section 4 defines hom_A(I),
+the ideal generated by homogeneous elements of I. Theorem 4.3 identifies it as
+an elimination intersection, and Procedure 1 enumerates a noncommutative
+Groebner basis. Section 5 similarly treats monomials in right ideals in Theorem
+5.4 and Procedure 2. The final paragraph leaves the arbitrary two-sided
+monomial-part problem open.
+
+The discriminating hardness question has a two-part answer. In general the
+paper's procedures enumerate possibly infinite Groebner bases and need not
+terminate; the Introduction explicitly ties this to undecidable free-algebra
+ideal membership. In this module all generators and the requested element have
+one fixed word length. Nonempty left or right multipliers raise that length, so
+membership in the requested homogeneous component is ordinary exact
+coefficient-space linear algebra. A Track A claim would therefore be false.
+This is Track B. Generic coefficient matching and Gaussian elimination is
+O(n^3). The strongest construction-aware mechanical method compares the target
+column with every candidate column and costs 2*n^2+n exact sign operations;
+selftest records its measured wall clock at the shipping preset. After the
+affine Walsh structure is noticed, a solver reads the images of 0 and the unit
+vectors, solves a log2(n)-dimensional binary system, and writes the character
+signs in fewer than 300 exact operations.
+
+Inverse generation chooses the answer word s, an invertible binary matrix A,
+an offset b, random row scales, and all auxiliary monomials first. For every
+binary word u it forms H_(Au+b)=M_u-N_u. The displayed ideal generators are a
+row-scaled Walsh transform of the H's. Orthogonality gives
+  (1/n) sum_z (-1)^(z dot (As+b)) G_z/scale_z = M_s-N_s.
+The distinguished C is set to N_s. Thus the certificate is carried through a
+structure-preserving invertible transformation and is never found by solving
+the generated instance. All terms have the same length, so the identity is a
+literal two-sided ideal-membership certificate over Q.
+
+Easy regimes and defenses. Right ideals with supplied finite generators have
+finite right Groebner bases by Lemma 2.1; zero-dimensional two-sided ideals are
+exactly the nonzero ideals that are finitely generated as right ideals in the
+equivalence theorem of Section 3. The module avoids presenting either a
+Groebner basis or the affine map. Plants and decoys are the same paired
+monomial construction, columns are shuffled, generators are shuffled and
+independently scaled, and auxiliary words are sampled uniformly subject only
+to uniqueness. The panel tests a normalized column-sum outlier, the
+lexicographically first candidate, 256 structure-aware random certificates,
+and the tempting identity-affine ansatz. The O(n^2) antipodal scan is reported
+honestly as the successful Track B reference algorithm, not mislabeled as a
+failed attack.
+
+canonical_key is invariant under generator reordering, monomial-column
+reordering, every permutation of the four free-algebra variable names, and
+their compositions. It does not quotient by arbitrary automorphisms of the
+free algebra or by invertible changes of the supplied generating set; computing
+a full ideal isomorphism invariant would recreate the hard algebraic problem.
+""".strip()
